# The DIRECT runtime dependencies, each pinned exactly. Edit this file, never
# requirements.txt: that is the lock compiled from it (H5, 1.1.6), with every
# package, transitive ones included, pinned and hashed. After an edit, recompile
# in the production platform (Linux, Python 3.12) from backend/:
#
#   docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/src" -w /src python:3.12-alpine sh -c \
#     "pip install -q pip-tools==7.6.2 && pip-compile --generate-hashes --strip-extras \
#      --allow-unsafe -q -o requirements.txt requirements.in && pip-compile \
#      --generate-hashes --strip-extras --allow-unsafe -q \
#      -o requirements-dev.txt requirements-dev.in"
#
# pip-compile downloads every published wheel of a package to hash it (several
# GB for Pillow, lxml and pydantic-core), but reuses the hashes already in the
# output file for versions that have not changed. So a bump is quick. A lock
# rebuilt from nothing is not: compile requirements.txt, copy it to
# requirements-dev.txt, then compile that.
#
# CI (ci/lock_check.py, in deps:audit) fails if a lock no longer matches this
# file. Bumps arrive as Dependabot PRs, which recompile both locks themselves.

# Core API
# fastapi, starlette and python-multipart were bumped together on 2026-10-09:
# pip-audit found 7 known vulnerabilities in each of starlette 0.37.2 and
# python-multipart 0.0.9 (fixed by 1.3.1 and 0.0.31). starlette is pinned
# directly because it is the package carrying the fixes; fastapi alone would
# accept anything >= 0.46.0. Starlette >= 0.40 caps form fields at 1 MB by
# default; app/services/form_limits.py raises that for metadata_json.
